---
import { Code } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import project from '../../.generated/project.json';
import example from '../../examples/use-after-free.c?raw';
const paths = [
  {
    number: '01',
    title: 'Run your first check',
    description: 'Install WeaveC, find a lifetime bug, and understand the fix.',
    href: '/getting-started/first-check/',
    label: 'GET STARTED',
  },
  {
    number: '02',
    title: 'Bring your codebase',
    description: 'Use your compilation database or build with weavec-cc.',
    href: '/guides/build-integration/',
    label: 'INTEGRATE',
  },
  {
    number: '03',
    title: 'Make the contract clear',
    description: 'Select functions for checked safety and inspect the evidence.',
    href: '/guides/checked-code/',
    label: 'GO DEEPER',
  },
];
---

<div class="home">
  <section class="home-hero" aria-labelledby="_top">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <a class="release-pill" href="/project/releases/"
        ><span class="pulse-dot"></span> WEAVEC v{project.version}<span class="pill-divider">/</span
        > EARLY RELEASE <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
      >
      <h1 id="_top">C, with more<br /><span>certainty.</span></h1>
      <p class="hero-description">
        Ownership and borrowing for the C you already write. Find memory bugs, make contracts
        explicit, and adopt checked safety one function at a time.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button-primary" href="/getting-started/installation/"
          >Get started <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a
        ><a class="button-secondary" href="/getting-started/introduction/"
          >Explore the docs <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
        >
      </div>
      <div class="hero-footnote"><span>BUILT ON CLANG + LLVM</span><span>OPEN SOURCE</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="hero-demo" aria-label="WeaveC catches a use after free through an alias">
      <div class="demo-label">
        <span class="demo-dot"></span> FOLLOW THE OWNERSHIP <span>C17</span>
      </div>
      <Code
        code={example}
        lang="c"
        title="example.c"
        mark="node_free(alias);"
        del="return n->value;"
      />
      <div class="diagnostic-output">
        <div>
          <span class="error-marker">ERROR</span><a href="/reference/diagnostics/use-after-free/"
            >weavec::use-after-free</a
          >
        </div><p>use of 'n' after it was freed</p><span class="diagnostic-note"
          >↳ freed here, through 'alias'</span
        >
      </div>
      <div class="demo-caption">
        <span aria-hidden="true">✓</span> Ownership inferred. No annotations needed for this example.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="principle-strip" aria-label="WeaveC principles">
    <span>YOUR C SOURCE</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><strong>INFER OWNERSHIP</strong><span
      aria-hidden="true">→</span
    ><strong>CHECK CONTRACTS</strong><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span>CLANG + LLVM</span>
  </div>
  <section class="home-section" aria-labelledby="start-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">A CLEAR PATH FORWARD</span><h2 id="start-title">
          Start small. Build confidence.
        </h2>
      </div><p>
        From one source file to a whole program.<br />Choose the next step for your project.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="path-grid">
      {
        paths.map((entry) => (
          <a class="path-card" href={entry.href}>
            <>
              <div class="card-top">
                <>
                  <span>{entry.label}</span>
                  <span class="card-number">{entry.number}</span>
                </>
              </div>
              <h3>{entry.title}</h3>
              <p>{entry.description}</p>
              <span class="card-arrow" aria-hidden="true">
                ↗
              </span>
            </>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="home-section model-section" aria-labelledby="model-title">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">A MODEL YOU CAN REASON ABOUT</span><h2 id="model-title">
        The intent is already<br />in your code.
      </h2><p>
        Allocators, cleanup functions, aliases, and control flow tell a story. WeaveC follows it
        across functions and source files.
      </p><a class="text-link" href="/guides/ownership/"
        >Understand ownership and borrowing <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a
      >
    </div>
    <div class="model-list">
      <div>
        <span>01</span><section>
          <h3>Infer first</h3><p>
            Function bodies reveal what they free, store, borrow, and return. Add annotations where
            an interface needs a contract.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div><div>
        <span>02</span><section>
          <h3>Keep your toolchain</h3><p>
            Analyze C with libTooling or use weavec-cc in a normal build. Clang handles parsing and
            code generation.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div><div>
        <span>03</span><section>
          <h3>Know the boundary</h3><p>
            Checked reports expose requirements, unresolved obligations, and trusted operations.
            Review the evidence behind each result.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="reference-band" aria-labelledby="reference-title">
    <div>
      <span class="eyebrow">WHEN YOU NEED THE DETAILS</span><h2 id="reference-title">
        Keep the reference close.
      </h2>
    </div><div class="reference-links">
      <a href="/reference/diagnostics/">Diagnostics <span>Understand the message ↗</span></a><a
        href="/reference/annotations/">Annotations <span>Express a contract ↗</span></a
      ><a href="/reference/cli/">Command line <span>Find the right flag ↗</span></a><a href="/rfcs/"
        >Design RFCs <span>Read the reasoning ↗</span></a
      >
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="scope-note">
    <span class="scope-icon" aria-hidden="true">i</span><p>
      <strong>Early software. Explicit guarantees.</strong> Checked safety applies to selected source
      functions within the supported model, under recorded requirements and trust boundaries. <a
        href="/reference/guarantees/">Read the scope and limits →</a
      >
    </p>
  </section>
</div>
